<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Biscay</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bizkaia, Vizcaya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Biscay (Basque: Bizkaia) is a province of the Basque Country in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a lordship ruled by the Haro family; it passed to the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1379 but kept its own fueros, or customary laws, and its assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guernica">Guernica</a> until the 19th century. Its iron mines and ports made it the industrial heart of the Basque Country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/biscay.jpg" alt="Biscay" width="320" height="275" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Biscay_in_Spain_(plus_Canarias).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Biscay lies on the south shore of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-biscay">Bay of Biscay</a> and is bordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cantabria">Cantabria</a> and the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgos">Burgos</a> to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gipuzkoa">Gipuzkoa</a> to the east and Álava to the south. The southern ranges of the Basque Mountains form a continuous barrier, with passes no lower than 600 metres, and the water divide between the Atlantic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> basins. The middle section is occupied by the valleys of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nervion">Nervión</a>, the Ibaizabal and the Kadagua. The coast is usually high, with cliffs and small inlets, and its main features are the estuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a> and the estuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guernica">Gernika</a>. The climate is oceanic, with high precipitation all year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Biscay has been inhabited since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-paleolithic">Middle Paleolithic</a>, and its caves hold cave paintings, those of Santimamiñe showing bison, horses, goats and deer. Roman geographers placed two tribes there, the Caristii and the Autrigones, who are thought to have spoken Basque. The Roman presence had little impact, and the Basque language and traditions survived. The name Biscay first appears in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-alfonso-iii">Chronicle of Alfonso III</a> in the late 9th century, and the territory was then controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre</a>.</p><p>The house of Haro, leaders of the pro-Castilian lords, was granted the rule of the newly created Biscay, and tradition names Iñigo López as its first Lord in 1043. The lordship later passed by inheritance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-of-castile">John I of Castile</a> and became one of the titles of the king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. Each lord had to swear to defend the fuero, the Biscayan laws derived from Navarrese and Basque customary rights, which held that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> lay with the Biscayans. Lords and later kings swore the statutes to the oak of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guernica">Gernika</a>, where the assembly of the lordship sits.</p><p>In 1511 the kings granted the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a> privileges for trade with the ports of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, and it was already the main Castilian harbour, shipping wool to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlist-wars">Carlist Wars</a> of the 19th century grew in part from the desire to keep foral rights, and the victory of the anti-Carlists brought successive cuts to Biscay's autonomy. Iron ore discovered in the 1850s brought foreign investment and rapid industrialisation. In 1937 German aircraft under Franco's control destroyed the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guernica">Gernika</a>, and Bilbao fell some months later. The 1979 Statute of Guernica created the Autonomous Community of the Basque Country.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Biscay is a province of the Basque Autonomous Community with its capital and largest city at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bilbao">Bilbao</a>. Its name is Bizkaia in Basque and Vizcaya in Spanish, and its institutions are the Juntas Generales and the Foral Diputation. Its population is 1,167,233.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Biscay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Spain was one of the most centralised countries in Europe: Franco abolished regional autonomy and the fueros of Guipuzcoa and <b>Biscay</b>, classed as &quot;traitor regions&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/empire/francoist-spain">Francoist Spain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The government controlled only <b>Biscay</b> and part of Gipuzkoa.</blockquote><a href="/state/euzkadi-1936-1937">Euzkadi (1936-1937)</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1040 Íñigo López Ezquerra appears in records as the first count or lord of <b>Biscay</b>, a vassal of Navarre.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-biscay">Lordship of Biscay</a></li><li><blockquote>He founded Durango in 1563 as its capital and named the province after his home region, <b>Biscay</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/nueva-vizcaya">Nueva Vizcaya</a></li></ul></section>
